1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,102
Four roommates.

2
00:00:01,602 --> 00:00:03,195
Forty small payments a month.

3
00:00:03,695 --> 00:00:05,826
And one question nobody can answer:

4
00:00:06,326 --> 00:00:07,077
who owes whom?

5
00:00:08,177 --> 00:00:16,491
In the next three minutes, I'll show you how to track every shared expense in your room, split it fairly, and settle up with UPI in one tap.

6
00:00:16,991 --> 00:00:18,808
We'll use a free app called Hisaab.

7
00:00:19,908 --> 00:00:20,916
Why does this matter?

8
00:00:21,416 --> 00:00:28,492
In a shared room, money moves in tiny amounts. A cab here, a food order there, the Wi-Fi recharge at the end of the month.

9
00:00:28,992 --> 00:00:31,790
Paying is easy with UPI. Remembering is not.

10
00:00:32,290 --> 00:00:38,657
And asking a friend for a hundred and twenty rupees feels awkward enough that most people just stop asking.

11
00:00:39,757 --> 00:00:44,372
When you open Hisaab for the first time, three short screens explain the idea.

12
00:00:44,872 --> 00:00:50,723
Add an expense in seconds. Split it any way that's fair. And settle up with any UPI app.

13
00:00:51,223 --> 00:00:57,284
Notice the promise on the last screen: Hisaab never holds your money, and never sees your UPI PIN.

14
00:00:57,784 --> 00:01:01,644
Then you sign in with your phone number, so your friends can find you.

15
00:01:02,743 --> 00:01:04,024
This is the home screen.

16
00:01:04,524 --> 00:01:08,500
At the top is one number: your overall balance across every group.

17
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,543
Here, I'm owed eight hundred and fifty four rupees in total.

18
00:01:13,043 --> 00:01:19,164
Below it are your groups. One for the hostel room, one for a trip, and one for a college project.

19
00:01:20,264 --> 00:01:24,708
Let's add an expense. Open the room group and tap the plus button.

20
00:01:25,208 --> 00:01:30,599
Type the amount: three hundred and sixty rupees for a late night Maggi and chai run.

21
00:01:31,099 --> 00:01:33,403
Diya paid, so we set paid by to Diya.

22
00:01:33,903 --> 00:01:38,024
Now tap split. By default it's split equally between everyone.

23
00:01:38,524 --> 00:01:41,568
But Aarav wasn't there. So we just untick him.

24
00:01:42,068 --> 00:01:49,162
Hisaab shows a hundred and twenty rupees each, and confirms the total still adds up. Tap done, then save.

25
00:01:50,262 --> 00:01:54,838
The expense appears at the top of the list, and everyone involved gets notified.

26
00:01:55,338 --> 00:01:57,393
Tap the balance card to see the details.

27
00:01:57,893 --> 00:02:04,023
Hisaab simplifies the debts: five separate payments become three, with exactly the same totals.

28
00:02:05,123 --> 00:02:07,102
Now the best part: settling up.

29
00:02:07,602 --> 00:02:12,474
Tap settle up. You see who you're paying, their UPI ID, and what it's for.

30
00:02:12,974 --> 00:02:18,140
Choose any UPI app, and confirm. The payment goes straight from your bank to theirs.

31
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:24,181
Done. Your balance updates instantly, and Hisaab even offers to pay the next person.

32
00:02:25,281 --> 00:02:26,643
Two more things worth knowing.

33
00:02:27,143 --> 00:02:32,950
If someone forgets to pay, you can send a friendly reminder on WhatsApp, in the tone you choose.

34
00:02:33,450 --> 00:02:37,119
And monthly insights show where your share of the money actually went.

35
00:02:37,619 --> 00:02:44,804
Pro tip: agree on one settle day every month, like the first. Then asking for money becomes a routine, not a favour.

36
00:02:45,904 --> 00:02:48,598
So that's it. Add, split, settle.

37
00:02:49,098 --> 00:02:52,144
No spreadsheets, and no awkward conversations.

38
00:02:52,644 --> 00:02:58,062
If this helped, subscribe for more student money tips, and I'll see you in the next one.
